WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.385
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:08.897
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:14.381
Unit ten: Metaphysics. Today you will discuss being using abstract noun subjects.

4
00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:17.391
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.589
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.660
Metaphysics.

7
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:29.784
Metaphysics means the branch of philosophy that studies the basic nature of reality.

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:34.136
Metaphysics asks what exists and why anything exists at all.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.762
Your turn. Say: Metaphysics.

10
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.729
Metaphysics.

11
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:43.717
Ontology.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:48.504
Ontology means the study of what exists and the categories of being.

13
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:53.310
Ontology asks whether numbers exist in the same way as tables.

14
00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:56.546
Your turn. Say: Ontology.

15
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.598
Ontology.

16
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.003
Being.

17
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:05.666
Being means existence, or the fact of existing.

18
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.668
Heidegger asked what it means for a being to be.

19
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:11.413
Your turn. Say: Being.

20
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:15.024
Being.

21
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:16.784
Essence.

22
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:20.863
Essence means the basic nature that makes something what it is.

23
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.158
Is there an essence that makes a human human?

24
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.135
Your turn. Say: Essence.

25
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.931
Essence.

26
00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:32.789
Existence.

27
00:01:33.783 --> 00:01:37.571
Existence means the fact of being real or present in the world.

28
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:40.810
Existence cannot be taken for granted.

29
00:01:41.417 --> 00:01:43.848
Your turn. Say: Existence.

30
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.933
Existence.

31
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:49.719
Causality.

32
00:01:50.717 --> 00:01:54.121
Causality means the relation between a cause and its effect.

33
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:57.750
Hume doubted that we ever observe causality directly.

34
00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.118
Your turn. Say: Causality.

35
00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:05.061
Causality.

36
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:07.038
Substance.

37
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.000
Substance means that which underlies and supports a thing's properties.

38
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:17.043
Aristotle believed a substance has properties but is not itself a property.

39
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.233
Your turn. Say: Substance.

40
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.277
Substance.

41
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:25.925
Reality.

42
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:30.507
Reality means the world as it truly is, apart from appearance.

43
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:34.765
Philosophers disagree about whether reality is mind-independent.

44
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.026
Your turn. Say: Reality.

45
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:41.937
Reality.

46
00:02:42.733 --> 00:02:46.431
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:52.699
Visal, why do philosophers keep asking whether things 'really' exist? A chair is a chair.

48
00:02:53.783 --> 00:03:03.936
Metaphysics questions what makes it a chair. Existence precedes essence, according to some philosophers, while others reverse the order.

49
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.295
Does the order matter?

50
00:03:07.383 --> 00:03:16.619
Enormously. Causality, identity and even freedom depend on the answer, because essence determines what a thing can become.

51
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:19.101
And who is right?

52
00:03:20.217 --> 00:03:29.998
Ontology offers no consensus. Reality may exceed every category we invent for it, which is precisely why the question endures.

53
00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:32.657
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:36.689
Grammar focus: Abstract noun subjects.

55
00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:38.210
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:42.634
Existence precedes essence, according to some philosophers.

57
00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.921
Essence determines what a thing can become.

58
00:03:46.817 --> 00:03:49.617
Reality may exceed every category we invent.

59
00:03:50.567 --> 00:04:00.524
Abstract nouns can act. Let ideas be the subject: Causality links events; Essence defines identity. This avoids 'I think' and sounds impersonal and academic.

60
00:04:01.283 --> 00:04:05.840
Be careful! Don't say: Existence is preceding essence, some say.

61
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.170
Say: Existence precedes essence, some say.

62
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.713
Use the simple present for claims.

63
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.048
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.833 --> 00:04:18.730
What Is There?.

65
00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:24.663
Metaphysics is the oldest branch of philosophy, concerned with the most general features of reality.

66
00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:34.354
Ontology, one of its central parts, asks what kinds of things exist: physical objects, numbers, minds, or perhaps only appearances.

67
00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:44.948
Aristotle held that every thing is a substance whose essence makes it what it is, whereas existentialists such as Sartre claimed that, for humans, existence precedes essence.

68
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:54.191
Causality poses a separate puzzle, since Hume argued that we observe sequence but never the necessary connection that makes one event produce another.

69
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.940
Such questions may seem remote, yet every scientific theory presupposes answers to them.

70
00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:05.209
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.060
blank precedes essence, according to Sartre.

72
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.686
A, Exist. B, Existence. C, Existing of.

73
00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:19.465
The answer is B: Existence.

74
00:05:20.617 --> 00:05:22.697
The study of what exists is blank.

75
00:05:22.997 --> 00:05:27.092
A, ontology. B, causality. C, essence.

76
00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:32.784
The answer is A: ontology.

77
00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:37.169
The basic nature that makes a thing what it is is its blank.

78
00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:41.145
A, essence. B, reality. C, being only.

79
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.479
The answer is A: essence.

80
00:05:47.617 --> 00:05:49.744
Choose the best academic sentence.

81
00:05:50.044 --> 00:05:56.739
A, I think reality is big.. B, Reality may exceed every category.. C, Reality, you know, is big..

82
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.041
The answer is B: Reality may exceed every category..

83
00:06:05.183 --> 00:06:07.821
According to the reading, Hume argued that we observe…

84
00:06:08.121 --> 00:06:13.458
A, necessary connections. B, sequence but not necessity. C, no events at all.

85
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.870
The answer is B: sequence but not necessity.

86
00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:24.241
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:28.499
What do you think is the relationship between essence and existence?

88
00:06:45.283 --> 00:06:46.795
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.095 --> 00:07:13.126
I find the existentialist view persuasive for humans: we exist first and define ourselves through choices. A person has no fixed essence waiting to be discovered. For objects, however, essence seems prior, since a knife is made for cutting before any particular knife exists. The relationship may therefore differ by kind of being. Reality seems richer than any single formula.

90
00:07:13.426 --> 00:07:15.321
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.217 --> 00:07:20.854
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and abstract noun subjects.

92
00:07:21.154 --> 00:07:26.419
Today's value is philosophical curiosity. Keep asking the big questions about life.

93
00:07:26.719 --> 00:07:29.290
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:32.289
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

